  "Z_tex": "Z = \\sum_{\\lambda,\\mu} |\\chi_{\\lambda,\\mu}|^2",
  "character_tex": "Frenkel-Kac-Wakimoto characters $\\chi_{\\lambda,\\mu} = \\eta(\\tau)^{-8}\\sum_{w\\in W}\\epsilon(w)\\sum_{\\alpha\\in Q} q^{|p'(\\lambda+\\rho)-p\\,w(\\mu+\\rho)+pp'\\alpha|^2/2pp'}$ of $\\mathcal{W}(E_8)$ at $(p,p') = (33,34)$, with $\\lambda$ an integrable $(E_8)_{3}$ weight and $\\mu$ an integrable $(E_8)_{4}$ weight (both labelled by the dimension of the $\\mathfrak{e_8}$ irrep), $h_{\\lambda,\\mu} = \\frac{|p'(\\lambda+\\rho)-p(\\mu+\\rho)|^2-|\\rho|^2}{2pp'}$. Equivalently $\\chi_{\\lambda,\\mu}$ is the branching function of the $(E_8)_{4}$ module $\\mu$ in the $(E_8)_1\\times(E_8)_{3}$ module $\\nu\\otimes\\lambda$, the level-one label $\\nu$ being fixed by the conjugacy classes of $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$. $E_8$ has trivial centre, so there is no field identification: $5\\times10 = 50$ fields.",
  "description": "Diagonal invariant of the coset $(E_8)_1\\times(E_8)_3/(E_8)_4$, the $\\mathcal{W}(E_8)$ minimal model (Bais-Bouwknegt-Surridge-Schoutens 1988; Frenkel-Kac-Wakimoto 1992). The affine Weyl orbit sum is done exactly by enumerating $E_8$ lattice points in a ball and folding them into the level-34 alcove (no sum over the finite Weyl group); the same code reproduces Rocha-Caridi and $\\mathcal{W}_3$ characters and independently computed $SU(4)$ and $SO(8)$ coset branching functions.",
